// facet/platform_sys_windows.cplus — the Windows half of the two primitives
// facet calls for itself. Shadows `platform_sys.cplus` through the resolver's
// `_windows` override.
//
// Same two questions, same contract; neither `pthread_self` nor `sched_yield`
// exists here, which is exactly why this file does.
//
// Both calls are kernel32, which is on the linker's default search path — so
// this needs no `[link]` section, the same way `stdlib/platform_sys_windows`
// reaches `GetActiveProcessorCount` without one.

// The calling thread's id. A DWORD, widened to the carrier the portable half
// uses — the value is only ever compared against another one taken the same
// way, so the width is storage and not meaning.
//
// `GetCurrentThreadId` and NOT `GetCurrentThread`: the latter answers a
// PSEUDO-HANDLE, the constant -1, which is the same value on every thread and
// would make M6's assertion pass everywhere. That is the failure mode this
// whole check exists to catch, so the wrong call here is worse than no check.
extern fn GetCurrentThreadId() -> u32;

// Yield the remainder of this thread's slice to another READY thread on the
// same processor, or answer false when there was none to yield to.
//
// The near neighbour is `Sleep(0)`, which will not release to a lower-priority
// thread — so a worker the main thread is waiting on can be starved by the
// wait itself. `SwitchToThread` has no such priority floor, which is what a
// teardown spin needs.
extern fn SwitchToThread() -> i32;

fn ui_thread_id() -> u64 { return { GetCurrentThreadId() } as u64; }

fn yield_once() { let _r: i32 = { SwitchToThread() }; return; }
